<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nobel Prize</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nobel Prizes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nobel Prizes are international awards founded under the will of the Swedish chemist and industrialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a> and first awarded in 1901. They are given each year for physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace, and since 1969 also for economic sciences, a prize established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s central bank. The prizes are presented in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, except for the peace prize, which is awarded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nobel-prize.jpg" alt="Nobel Prize" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nobel Foundation, diploma by Sofia Gisberg[1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marie_Sk%C5%82odowska-Curie%27s_Nobel_Prize_in_Chemistry_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a> was born on 21 October 1833 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> into a family of engineers. He was a chemist, engineer and inventor who amassed a fortune from 355 inventions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynamite">dynamite</a> among them. In 1894 he purchased the Bofors iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> mill, which he made into a major armaments manufacturer. A popular story holds that in 1888 he read his own obituary, titled The Merchant of Death Is Dead, in a French newspaper, when it was in fact his brother Ludvig who had died, and that this inspired him to change his will. Historians have been unable to verify the story, and some dismiss it as a myth.</p><p>Nobel died on 10 December 1896 in his villa in San Remo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, of a cerebral haemorrhage, aged 63. His last will was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 27 November 1895. To widespread astonishment it specified that his fortune be used for prizes to those who confer the most benefit on mankind in physics, chemistry, physiology or medicine, literature and peace. He left 94 per cent of his assets, 31 million SEK, to establish the five prizes. Because of scepticism about the will, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> did not approve it until 26 April 1897, and its executors, Ragnar Sohlman and Rudolf Lilljequist, formed the Nobel Foundation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The prizes were first awarded in 1901. A sixth prize, in Economic Sciences, was established in 1968 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sveriges-riksbank">Sveriges Riksbank</a> in memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a> and has been awarded since 1969. Except in special circumstances such as war, all are given annually. Each laureate receives a medal, a diploma and a monetary award. No more than three individuals may share a prize, though the Peace Prize can go to organisations. Prizes are not awarded posthumously, but one is presented if its winner dies after being named.</p><p>The process is similar for all prizes. Nomination forms go to about 3,000 individuals, generally prominent academics, with the deadline on 31 January. The committee nominates about 300 potential laureates, and nominees are not named publicly or told, and all nomination records are sealed for 50 years.</p><p>Four institutions choose the laureates by majority vote, and their decision cannot be appealed: the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences for chemistry, physics and economics, the Nobel Assembly at the Karolinska Institute for physiology or medicine, the Swedish Academy for literature and the Norwegian Nobel Committee for peace. Winners are announced during the first two weeks of October.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nobel Prize” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cultural life flourished among the new middle classes: Rabindranath Tagore won the <b>Nobel Prize</b> in Literature in 1913, and the architecture of New Delhi, designed by Edwin Lutyens and Herbert Baker and inaugurated in 1931, still houses the government of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Australian scientists and writers have had wide influence, including Howard Florey, who shared the <b>Nobel Prize</b> in 1945 for the development of penicillin, and the novelist Patrick White, Nobel laureate in 1973.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hungary's intellectual weight outlasts its size: thirteen Hungarian scientists had received <b>Nobel Prizes</b> by 2018, the country spent 1.61% of GDP on research in 2020 and ranks fourth in the all-time medal count of the International Mathematical Olympiad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In culture the national poet Muhammad Iqbal shaped Urdu letters, Abdus Salam won the <b>Nobel Prize</b> in Physics and Malala Yousafzai became the youngest Nobel laureate; Pakistan launched the first satellite of any Muslim nation in 1990 and has won every major ICC cricket tournament.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Swedish scientists included Carl Linnaeus, whose Systema Naturae (1735) founded modern biological classification, and Anders Celsius, and the will of Alfred Nobel created the <b>Nobel Prizes</b>, first awarded in 1901.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Chilean literature gained world recognition: Gabriela Mistral became the first Latin American winner of the <b>Nobel Prize</b> in Literature in 1945, and Pablo Neruda followed in 1971.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
